---
/* Wrapper page for examples/raw/landing.html.
 *
 * The artifact is NOT rendered by Astro and never will be. It is a standalone
 * document -- that is what makes it something a client can be handed -- and a
 * documentation bar welded to the top would stop it being one. So this page is
 * the documentation and the <iframe> below is the artifact, in its own
 * document, at its own width.
 *
 * That split is what lets both rules hold at once: every ROUTE on this site
 * carries the same menu, and every ARTIFACT stays the thing it demonstrates.
 *
 * GENERATED once from a table and authored from here on. Edit this file.
 */
import DocPage from '../../layouts/DocPage.astro';
---
<DocPage
  title="CODECAVE — Landing page"
  nav="examples"
  sub="landing"
  stylesheets={['examples.css']}
>
<div class="ex">

  <header class="ex-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Web</p>
    <h1>Landing page</h1>
    <p class="ex-lead">A full marketing page built from the system — hero, proof, services, testimonial and a closing CTA.</p>
    <p class="ex-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="raw/landing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open standalone</a>
      <code>examples/raw/landing.html</code>
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="ex-frame" data-ratio="tall">
    <iframe src="raw/landing.html" title="Landing page" loading="lazy"></iframe>
  </div>

  <section class="ex-notes">
    <h2>The most compliant artifact of the six</h2>
    <p>It reads almost entirely through tokens — the type ramp, the radius scale and the semantic colour layer — rather than restating values. If you want to see what "built from the system" means concretely, read this one first.</p>
    <h2>It draws its own nav, and that is correct</h2>
    <p>The bar inside the frame is <em>content</em>: a landing page has a header. It is not this documentation site’s bar, which is above the frame, outside the artifact.</p>
  </section>

</div>
</DocPage>
